  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes material strategies for improving a wearable or skin-mounted electrochemical sensor. A patch-like device on the left is linked to four design levels that affect sensing performance.\n\nThe first level focuses on the electrode itself, showing plain electrode materials, surfaces modified with self-assembled monolayers, and functional nanomaterials. These additions imply better control of electron transfer and more sites for attaching sensing components. The second level highlights engineered electrode surfaces, especially porous textures, which increase loading capability and active area and therefore enhance signal.\n\nA third approach uses hydrogel or polymer coatings on top of the sensor. These coatings appear to help immobilize target molecules or recognition elements, improve stability, protect against biofouling, and provide size or charge selectivity. Together, these functions suggest cleaner and more reliable sensing in complex biological fluids.\n\nThe final level addresses mechanical design, showing flexible or stretchable materials such as elastomers, mesh structures, and serpentine patterns. These layouts are intended to let the sensor bend and stretch with the body while maintaining function.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that sensor performance depends on a combination of electrode chemistry, surface architecture, protective coatings, and mechanical compliance.",
